WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.390
<v Speaker 1>Welcome to the debate.

2
00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:09.070
<v Speaker 1>Imagine standing in the freezing cold shivering and just trying to warm yourself by repeating the word fire over and over again.

3
00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:11.390
<v Speaker 2>I mean, that sounds completely absurd.

4
00:00:11.390 --> 00:00:12.270
<v Speaker 1>It does, yeah.

5
00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:20.270
<v Speaker 1>But according to a profoundly challenging medieval text we are exploring today, that is exactly what most of us are doing when we talk about the divine.

6
00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:25.550
<v Speaker 1>The text is called The Master and the Disciple, written by a brilliant medieval author.

7
00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:30.670
<v Speaker 1>And we are focusing on a very specific, deeply unsettling teaching from it.

8
00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:38.670
<v Speaker 1>The text proposes that the school's standard creed about the Creator's unity does not actually grasp the reality the words point to.

9
00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.270
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, it's a huge claim.

10
00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:41.150
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

11
00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:46.910
<v Speaker 1>So to frame our entire exchange, I will state the central thesis of the Master in this text verbatim.

12
00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:51.950
<v Speaker 1>names and descriptions are of no benefit without their essential realities.

13
00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:52.990
<v Speaker 2>Very powerful.

14
00:00:53.230 --> 00:01:04.270
<v Speaker 1>I will be representing the perspective of the master, arguing that reciting theological words without accessing the reality they point to leaves a community with absolutely nothing.

15
00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:07.710
<v Speaker 2>And I will be representing the perspective of the disciple.

16
00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:16.430
<v Speaker 2>I am defending the utility of shared vocabulary, of creed, and of the theological structures we use to understand the divine.

17
00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:17.710
<v Speaker 1>Which makes sense.

18
00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:20.910
<v Speaker 1>It is how human institutions generally operate.

19
00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:28.590
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, from the disciple's view, having a correct, transmissible doctrine is the absolute core requirement of faith.

20
00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:35.790
<v Speaker 2>We need words to package doctrine so it can be preserved, taught to our children, and understood across generations.

21
00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:40.110
<v Speaker 1>So how does the disciple articulate this core requirement initially?

22
00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:43.790
<v Speaker 2>Well, the disciple begins from a moment of crisis.

23
00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:47.870
<v Speaker 2>Though he initially views it as a moment of absolute confidence.

24
00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:54.110
<v Speaker 2>He recites A precise four-line creed to summarize his entire theological universe.

25
00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:55.070
<v Speaker 1>Let's hear it.

26
00:01:55.310 --> 00:02:01.790
<v Speaker 2>He states that the Creator is one, is just, commands by giving a choice, and forbids by giving warning.

27
00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:05.630
<v Speaker 2>From the disciples' perspective, the conversation should just end right there.

28
00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:06.910
<v Speaker 1>He thinks he solved it.

29
00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:07.790
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

30
00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:11.390
<v Speaker 2>This is a complete and sufficient theological structure to him.

31
00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:13.870
<v Speaker 2>The words package the doctrine perfectly.

32
00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:19.790
<v Speaker 2>By memorizing those four lines, the disciple believes he has adequately comprehended the nature of the Creator.

33
00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:22.590
<v Speaker 1>I see why you think that, but let me give you a different perspective.

34
00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:26.270
<v Speaker 1>The master entirely refuses this shared vocabulary.

35
00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:26.750
<v Speaker 2>Really?

36
00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:28.110
<v Speaker 2>Completely refuses it.

37
00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:28.990
<v Speaker 1>Completely.

38
00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:36.830
<v Speaker 1>He argues that knowing the textbook definition of monotheism, what the school calls tawhid, is not the same as knowing the reality.

39
00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:40.990
<v Speaker 1>The master deploys a very specific analogy to explain this failure.

40
00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:43.310
<v Speaker 1>Think of fire and food.

41
00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:48.110
<v Speaker 1>If you say the word fire, that vocalization alone cooks no food.

42
00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:49.870
<v Speaker 1>It brightens no darkness.

43
00:02:50.350 --> 00:03:00.110
<v Speaker 1>A community that possesses only the word fire without actual access to combustion and heat is just a community standing in the cold, shivering while repeating A syllable.

44
00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:01.550
<v Speaker 2>That is a stark image.

45
00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:02.270
<v Speaker 1>It is.

46
00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:07.310
<v Speaker 1>And the master looks at the disciples' four-line creed and sees the exact same phenomenon.

47
00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:15.470
<v Speaker 1>The community can chant the words, they can memorize the definitions, but if they do not access the reality those words point to, they have nothing at all.

48
00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:17.230
<v Speaker 2>I come at it from a different way, though.

49
00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:22.990
<v Speaker 2>We have to look closely at the intellectual labor the school has invested in that vocabulary.

50
00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:28.750
<v Speaker 2>The school has produced rigorous, generation-spanning scholarship on the divine name.

51
00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:35.630
<v Speaker 2>Scholars have labored immensely over the exact structure, the visible form of the letters, the precise definitions.

52
00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:37.630
<v Speaker 1>They certainly have spent a lot of time on it.

53
00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:40.910
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, and they have not done this frivolously.

54
00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:46.190
<v Speaker 2>They have done this because words and letters are the precise instruments of human cognition.

55
00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:52.750
<v Speaker 2>The forms of letters themselves are the instruments human beings use to relate to the divine.

56
00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:56.190
<v Speaker 1>Well, I am not convinced by that line of reasoning.

57
00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:01.070
<v Speaker 1>because the Master applies immense pressure to exactly that defense.

58
00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:05.470
<v Speaker 1>He asks the disciple to look at the four letters of the name Allah.

59
00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:07.310
<v Speaker 2>Okay, looking at the letters.

60
00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:10.670
<v Speaker 1>The Master asks a devastatingly literal question.

61
00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:13.470
<v Speaker 1>Which of those 4 letters is the Creator?

62
00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:17.470
<v Speaker 1>Are you suggesting the first letter holds the reality or the last?

63
00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:19.310
<v Speaker 2>Well, obviously neither in isolation.

64
00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:21.870
<v Speaker 1>But if they are separated, which one is the divine?

65
00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:28.430
<v Speaker 1>If they are joined together in a specific configuration, which exact configuration holds the essential reality?

66
00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:32.110
<v Speaker 1>The Master's point is that the letters must be signs pointing outward.

67
00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:35.390
<v Speaker 1>They cannot be containers holding the reality inside them.

68
00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:37.950
<v Speaker 2>I'm sorry, but I just don't buy that.

69
00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:39.390
<v Speaker 2>Let me tell you why.

70
00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:41.310
<v Speaker 2>That sounds like word play.

71
00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:42.350
<v Speaker 1>Word play.

72
00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:42.910
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

73
00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:49.550
<v Speaker 2>Pressing on the physical forms of individual letters feels like a rhetorical trick to confuse the disciple.

74
00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:56.510
<v Speaker 2>If the creed is correct as a conceptual framework, why does saying the creator is one not count?

75
00:04:56.590 --> 00:04:58.670
<v Speaker 1>Because the word is not the reality.

76
00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:02.110
<v Speaker 2>But the disciple is not claiming the ink on the page is the creator.

77
00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:07.550
<v Speaker 2>He is claiming the concept conveyed by the word one is accurate and necessary.

78
00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:12.670
<v Speaker 2>Why dismiss the entire framework just because the letters are merely physical signs?

79
00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:15.870
<v Speaker 1>Trick, and it is not mere wordplay.

80
00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:20.830
<v Speaker 1>The master is exposing A fundamental error in how the disciples' school operates.

81
00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:22.150
<v Speaker 2>Which is what, exactly?

82
00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:27.230
<v Speaker 1>That the grammar of the name does not correspond to the divine reality it points to.

83
00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:33.790
<v Speaker 1>The master is pointing out that the school has become obsessed with the signs and has totally forgotten what the signs are for.

84
00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:34.670
<v Speaker 2>I see.

85
00:05:35.230 --> 00:05:42.110
<v Speaker 1>Ritual form, textbook definitions, and grammatical perfection have completely displaced the actual reality.

86
00:05:42.510 --> 00:05:50.270
<v Speaker 1>When they argue endlessly over the pronunciation of a syllable, they are studying the physical shape of the letters themselves while freezing to death.

87
00:05:50.590 --> 00:05:57.230
<v Speaker 2>Even if we accept the limitation of letters, the disciple is a careful thinker who has a fallback position.

88
00:05:57.550 --> 00:05:59.230
<v Speaker 1>The manufacture argument, yes.

89
00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:00.110
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

90
00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:02.910
<v Speaker 2>The disciple pivots to the manufacture argument.

91
00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:07.630
<v Speaker 2>This is an incredibly careful, honest, and ubiquitous theological move.

92
00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:10.830
<v Speaker 2>The disciple argues that every made thing has a maker.

93
00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:12.190
<v Speaker 1>A classic sequence.

94
00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:22.590
<v Speaker 2>When we observe the immense order of the universe, the precision of celestial bodies, the complexity of biological life, that physical order must logically point to a creator.

95
00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:28.910
<v Speaker 2>We do not need the letters to contain the divine because the manufactured universe itself serves as the physical evidence.

96
00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:34.350
<v Speaker 1>But the master deconstructs this logical assumption with absolute precision.

97
00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:38.590
<v Speaker 1>He issues a non-bodily correction to this idea of the maker.

98
00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:39.310
<v Speaker 2>How does he do that?

99
00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:44.030
<v Speaker 1>He uses another specific analogy to demonstrate why this fails.

100
00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:46.430
<v Speaker 1>Think of the craftsman and his thing.

101
00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:50.430
<v Speaker 1>A worldly craftsman makes things from a pre-existing substance.

102
00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:52.030
<v Speaker 2>Like a carpenter needing wood.

103
00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:52.910
<v Speaker 1>Precisely.

104
00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:56.110
<v Speaker 1>A carpenter needs wood, a smith needs iron.

105
00:06:56.430 --> 00:07:02.030
<v Speaker 1>The act of manufacturing is essentially the act of rearranging materials that already exist.

106
00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:06.590
<v Speaker 1>but the creator creates from absolutely no substance.

107
00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:10.910
<v Speaker 1>Therefore, the craftsman model never applied in the 1st place.

108
00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:17.550
<v Speaker 1>You cannot extrapolate the nature of the creator by observing how a craftsman manipulates existing materials.

109
00:07:17.790 --> 00:07:24.830
<v Speaker 1>The fundamental act of creation from nothing shares no mechanics whatsoever with the act of manufacturing from something.

110
00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:27.710
<v Speaker 2>That is a severe restriction on human reasoning though.

111
00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:29.070
<v Speaker 1>It is meant to be.

112
00:07:29.230 --> 00:07:37.070
<v Speaker 1>The disciple uses the craftsman argument because it is the most logical cognitive sequence available to the human mind.

113
00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:47.070
<v Speaker 1>If you discard it because of the issue of pre-existing substance, you strip the disciple of the ability to infer anything at all about the creator from the created world.

114
00:07:47.390 --> 00:07:49.950
<v Speaker 1>That is exactly the master's intention.

115
00:07:50.830 --> 00:07:54.190
<v Speaker 1>Because the craftsman argument is actually dangerous.

116
00:07:54.670 --> 00:08:00.910
<v Speaker 1>Arguing from made things to a maker inevitably forges a hidden likeness.

117
00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:02.110
<v Speaker 1>The hidden likeness?

118
00:08:02.110 --> 00:08:02.670
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

119
00:08:03.230 --> 00:08:12.910
<v Speaker 1>When the human mind uses the craftsman model, it cannot help but imagine a being with a location, a geometry, and a method of physical manipulation.

120
00:08:13.390 --> 00:08:23.070
<v Speaker 1>Even when the school tries to protect itself by using the popular formula, nothing like his image, it secretly smuggles an image back under the table.

121
00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:24.430
<v Speaker 2>By using the word image.

122
00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:25.070
<v Speaker 1>Correct.

123
00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:32.670
<v Speaker 1>The very use of the word image, even in a negative context, forces the human mind to consider a geometry, a boundary.

124
00:08:33.390 --> 00:08:39.630
<v Speaker 1>To correct this, the master notes that the text of the Quran explicitly states, there is none like him.

125
00:08:40.030 --> 00:08:40.990
<v Speaker 2>No exceptions.

126
00:08:41.230 --> 00:08:42.190
<v Speaker 1>No exceptions.

127
00:08:42.590 --> 00:08:45.950
<v Speaker 1>That is a refusal of similitude in a single clause.

128
00:08:46.430 --> 00:08:49.790
<v Speaker 1>It leaves no noun to which a comparison could be salvaged.

129
00:08:50.110 --> 00:08:53.790
<v Speaker 1>And this brings us back to the central spine of the Master's instruction.

130
00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:58.270
<v Speaker 1>Names and descriptions are of no benefit without their essential realities.

131
00:08:58.510 --> 00:09:02.830
<v Speaker 2>How is this different from any negative theology that just denies attributes?

132
00:09:03.550 --> 00:09:07.870
<v Speaker 2>If we strip away the craftsman model, how do we speak about the divine at all?

133
00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:09.550
<v Speaker 1>It is a profound problem.

134
00:09:09.550 --> 00:09:16.190
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, if every attempt to describe the creator is a smuggled likeness, we are left entirely mute.

135
00:09:16.590 --> 00:09:20.990
<v Speaker 2>The disciples framework at least gave a community a method to speak and gather.

136
00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:26.270
<v Speaker 2>If we dismantle that, we are dismantling the very structure of theological education.

137
00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:29.950
<v Speaker 1>That is an interesting point, though I would frame it differently.

138
00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:36.350
<v Speaker 1>The master would argue that dismantling a false structure is the strict prerequisite for encountering reality.

139
00:09:36.430 --> 00:09:37.630
<v Speaker 2>So where do we go from there?

140
00:09:37.950 --> 00:09:47.870
<v Speaker 1>To address exactly how we speak about the divine, the master relies on the profound wisdom of the commander of the faithful, peace be upon him, in the book, The Peak of Eloquence.

141
00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:49.870
<v Speaker 2>Okay, what is the instruction?

142
00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:52.670
<v Speaker 1>The instruction provided there is incredibly precise.

143
00:09:52.910 --> 00:09:55.870
<v Speaker 1>Attaching attributes limits and numbers the divine.

144
00:09:56.270 --> 00:10:00.030
<v Speaker 1>Every single attribute bears witness that it is other than the thing described.

145
00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:02.590
<v Speaker 2>I need you to explain the mechanics of that.

146
00:10:02.750 --> 00:10:06.670
<v Speaker 2>What does it mean that an attribute numbers the divine?

147
00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:10.110
<v Speaker 1>Let us look at the mechanics of human language.

148
00:10:10.190 --> 00:10:15.310
<v Speaker 2>If I say a leader is wise, I am just acknowledging their qualities, right?

149
00:10:15.550 --> 00:10:17.390
<v Speaker 2>I mean, acknowledging what they are.

150
00:10:17.630 --> 00:10:20.510
<v Speaker 2>How does attaching an attribute divide the subject?

151
00:10:20.510 --> 00:10:26.590
<v Speaker 1>Well, if you say the creator is powerful, you have immediately created a duality in your mind.

152
00:10:26.990 --> 00:10:32.270
<v Speaker 1>You have the essence of the creator and you have the distinct concept of power attached to it.

153
00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:33.630
<v Speaker 2>Oh, I see.

154
00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:35.070
<v Speaker 2>Two separate things.

155
00:10:35.070 --> 00:10:35.790
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

156
00:10:36.350 --> 00:10:39.310
<v Speaker 1>Are the essence and the power the exact same thing?

157
00:10:39.630 --> 00:10:43.470
<v Speaker 1>If they are the exact same thing, the word power is redundant.

158
00:10:43.710 --> 00:10:48.670
<v Speaker 1>If they are not the exact same thing, you have turned absolute unity into a pairing.

159
00:10:48.910 --> 00:10:50.750
<v Speaker 1>You have two distinct realities.

160
00:10:50.750 --> 00:10:52.430
<v Speaker 2>So you divide the unity.

161
00:10:52.510 --> 00:10:52.990
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

162
00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:55.630
<v Speaker 1>As the commander of the faithful explains in that sermon,

163
00:10:56.030 --> 00:10:59.070
<v Speaker 1>Whoever attaches attributes pairs the divine.

164
00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:02.110
<v Speaker 1>Whoever pairs the divine divides the divine.

165
00:11:02.270 --> 00:11:05.790
<v Speaker 1>And whoever divides the divine limits and numbers the divine.

166
00:11:05.950 --> 00:11:09.310
<v Speaker 2>Because the intellect slices reality into pieces.

167
00:11:09.310 --> 00:11:10.750
<v Speaker 1>You hit the nail on the head.

168
00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:16.190
<v Speaker 1>The human intellect uses attributes to slice reality into comprehensible pieces.

169
00:11:16.590 --> 00:11:20.430
<v Speaker 1>But the creator cannot be sliced, contrasted, or numbered.

170
00:11:20.830 --> 00:11:25.470
<v Speaker 1>Therefore, the intellect's standard method of operation completely fails.

171
00:11:25.510 --> 00:11:31.390
<v Speaker 2>That's A compelling argument, but have you considered the psychological and communal cost of this position?

172
00:11:31.630 --> 00:11:33.390
<v Speaker 1>It is definitely a high cost.

173
00:11:33.470 --> 00:11:43.950
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, if we cannot point, cannot describe, cannot attach attributes without dividing the absolute unity, aren't we leaving the intellect completely bewildered and paralyzed?

174
00:11:44.590 --> 00:11:48.190
<v Speaker 2>You are asking the disciple to abandon the tools of reason and language.

175
00:11:48.350 --> 00:11:57.310
<v Speaker 1>The master introduces a final analogy to explain why this paralysis is actually the correct and necessary state of the intellect.

176
00:11:58.110 --> 00:11:59.550
<v Speaker 1>Think of the sun and the eye.

177
00:12:00.030 --> 00:12:01.710
<v Speaker 2>Okay, the sun and the eye.

178
00:12:02.510 --> 00:12:10.270
<v Speaker 1>The human eye is a remarkable physical instrument designed to perceive the physical world by detecting differences in light, shadows and boundaries.

179
00:12:10.670 --> 00:12:14.750
<v Speaker 1>but the eye cannot bear even the lesser light of the sun without breaking down.

180
00:12:14.830 --> 00:12:15.790
<v Speaker 2>It gets overwhelmed.

181
00:12:15.790 --> 00:12:16.510
<v Speaker 1>Completely.

182
00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:22.430
<v Speaker 1>If you stare directly at the sun, an absolute source of light, the physical instrument of your vision is destroyed.

183
00:12:22.910 --> 00:12:25.710
<v Speaker 1>Consequently, the intellect cannot weigh the one.

184
00:12:26.030 --> 00:12:29.630
<v Speaker 1>The faculties of the human mind have a strict absolute limit.

185
00:12:30.030 --> 00:12:31.710
<v Speaker 2>So the intellect is like the eye.

186
00:12:31.790 --> 00:12:36.110
<v Speaker 1>Yes, the intellect is an instrument designed to parse boundaries and differences.

187
00:12:36.430 --> 00:12:43.150
<v Speaker 1>When you apply an instrument designed for boundaries to an entity that has absolutely no boundaries, the instrument physically and conceptually breaks down.

188
00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:45.150
<v Speaker 2>So the bewilderment is natural.

189
00:12:45.390 --> 00:12:49.390
<v Speaker 1>It is exactly like the retina breaking down when it tries to process the sun.

190
00:12:49.710 --> 00:12:52.350
<v Speaker 1>It is not a failure of the seeker's devotion.

191
00:12:52.590 --> 00:12:56.910
<v Speaker 1>It is a structural, mechanical limitation of the cognitive instrument itself.

192
00:12:57.230 --> 00:12:59.630
<v Speaker 2>That is an incredibly heavy realization.

193
00:12:59.630 --> 00:13:00.910
<v Speaker 1>It changes everything.

194
00:13:00.990 --> 00:13:06.070
<v Speaker 2>The disciple has spent a lifetime building a theological vocabulary, assuming those

195
00:13:06.190 --> 00:13:09.070
<v Speaker 2>words were valid instruments for reaching the divine.

196
00:13:09.630 --> 00:13:12.750
<v Speaker 2>Now the master is demonstrating that the words are empty letters.

197
00:13:13.070 --> 00:13:15.150
<v Speaker 2>The craftsman model is a hidden idol.

198
00:13:15.230 --> 00:13:20.990
<v Speaker 1>The master systematically invalidates every logical sequence the disciple tries to use.

199
00:13:21.150 --> 00:13:25.070
<v Speaker 2>I have to admit, looking at it through the disciple's perspective is exhausting.

200
00:13:25.550 --> 00:13:29.630
<v Speaker 2>I see the absolute exhaustion of the intellectual frameworks I have defended.

201
00:13:29.710 --> 00:13:33.150
<v Speaker 1>The physical signs of the letters cannot contain the reality.

202
00:13:33.310 --> 00:13:34.510
<v Speaker 2>No, they cannot.

203
00:13:34.910 --> 00:13:38.910
<v Speaker 2>And the craftsman model falsely assumes pre-existing material.

204
00:13:39.470 --> 00:13:44.510
<v Speaker 2>The attributes inevitably divide in number what must remain absolute unity.

205
00:13:45.150 --> 00:13:50.910
<v Speaker 2>Given all of this mechanical failure of human cognition, I must offer the disciples concession.

206
00:13:50.910 --> 00:13:51.630
<v Speaker 1>Which is.

207
00:13:51.870 --> 00:13:53.230
<v Speaker 2>My words have left me.

208
00:13:53.950 --> 00:13:59.150
<v Speaker 2>I confess that intellects are bewildered and imaginations cannot reach the Creator.

209
00:13:59.710 --> 00:14:03.870
<v Speaker 2>I acknowledge that the names have been emptied of the function they were being used for.

210
00:14:04.430 --> 00:14:09.070
<v Speaker 2>The creed I recited with such confidence at the beginning is just a collection of syllables.

211
00:14:09.230 --> 00:14:12.430
<v Speaker 1>The Master validates this exact bewilderment.

212
00:14:12.750 --> 00:14:15.470
<v Speaker 1>This paralysis of the intellect is not a failure.

213
00:14:15.710 --> 00:14:18.750
<v Speaker 1>It is the honest, factual destination of the seeker.

214
00:14:19.070 --> 00:14:20.990
<v Speaker 2>To just stand in that bewilderment.

215
00:14:20.990 --> 00:14:29.310
<v Speaker 1>Yes, realizing that your words and your logical analogies have structurally failed is the first moment of actual truth.

216
00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:33.550
<v Speaker 1>You have stopped mistaking the word fire for the heat itself.

217
00:14:33.870 --> 00:14:37.230
<v Speaker 1>You have accepted the mechanical limits of the human cognitive instrument.

218
00:14:37.470 --> 00:14:39.710
<v Speaker 2>The resulting silence is daunting, though.

219
00:14:40.030 --> 00:14:48.590
<v Speaker 2>For a community that relies on discourse, admitting that our descriptions literally cannot reach the reality forces a complete reevaluation of practice.

220
00:14:48.670 --> 00:14:56.910
<v Speaker 1>It demands immense humility, and it shifts the burden of proof from reciting the right syllables to examining our actual state.

221
00:14:57.550 --> 00:15:01.790
<v Speaker 1>We must close by turning the stakes directly to the listener with a heavy question.

222
00:15:01.790 --> 00:15:02.830
<v Speaker 2>What is the question?

223
00:15:02.950 --> 00:15:07.070
<v Speaker 1>Which creed words am I reciting while having lost the reality behind them?

224
00:15:07.390 --> 00:15:09.630
<v Speaker 1>And what would it cost this week to stop?

225
00:15:10.190 --> 00:15:15.950
<v Speaker 1>This brings us to our final reflection, landing our central thesis one last time verbatim.

226
00:15:16.430 --> 00:15:20.510
<v Speaker 1>Names and descriptions are of no benefit without their essential realities.

227
00:15:20.910 --> 00:15:25.550
<v Speaker 2>It leaves us contemplating the silence that follows when the names are finally set down.

228
00:15:25.870 --> 00:15:28.590
<v Speaker 2>and recognizing the structural limits of our own minds.

229
00:15:28.830 --> 00:15:35.150
<v Speaker 1>Indeed, we leave you with that thought to sit in the cold and ask whether you possess the fire or merely the word.
